neural-like/evolved behavior trees

use parallel nodes to combine results of sensory action nodes
then use those outputs to control actions - setting direction, choosing message to send
in effect, this is a ff neural net with binary output functions - is that expressive enough?

parallel nodes will be the bread and butter
- execute all children at once
- if more than n children return success, you return success
- thresholds of the parallel nodes are what we evolve

- also need a limited set of actuation nodes
- limited set of sensing nodes

evolution:
two parameters:
- growth chance (expand the behavior tree)
- mutation chance (modify a parameter in a node)
- deletion chance (drop a node and all its children)
start with an empty node (brain = None)
then at each evolution step:
- do dfs starting at root of btree
- at each node:
    - roll for deletion: if successful and this node is not the root, delete this node and continue to next node
    - roll for mutation: if successful, mutate this node node
    - roll for growth: if successful and this node is a control node, add a random randomly initialized child node

when agents reproduce, do a deep copy of their behavior tree and apply evolution to it

